---
const stack = [
  {
    group: 'AI & vision',
    items: ['PyTorch', 'timm', 'scikit-learn', 'SciPy', 'OpenCV', 'Segment Anything', 'ONNX Runtime'],
  },
  {
    group: 'Models',
    items: ['EfficientNetV2', 'ViT', 'CAFormer', 'ConvNeXt', 'YuNet', 'SFace'],
  },
  {
    group: 'Software',
    items: ['Python', 'NumPy', 'pandas', 'FastAPI', 'Pydantic', 'Git', 'Google Colab', 'Kaggle GPUs'],
  },
  {
    group: 'Hardware & embedded',
    items: ['Raspberry Pi 4B', 'STM32F411', 'RS-485', 'Modbus RTU', 'I2C', 'KiCad'],
  },
];
---

<section id="about" aria-labelledby="about-title">
  <h2 class="label" id="about-title"><span class="no">01</span> about</h2>
  <p>
    I like the layer where software meets the physical world: buses, power, timing, and the failures that follow. On
    the research side I treat every claim as something that has to be measured, not asserted.
  </p>

  <h3 class="sub mono">How I work</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Measure before fixing.</strong> Rules come from measurements on real data, not hypotheses. Each one ships
      with a test that reproduces the observed case.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Prove a component matters.</strong> Before training, randomize a branch and check that the output
      changes. A check like this exposed a dead 85.8M-parameter branch.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Report what was measured.</strong> Fixed and logged seeds, multiple runs, bootstrap confidence
      intervals, multiple-comparison correction, predictions written down before each run, negative results kept.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>System before model; safety in hardware.</strong> Cut compute at the system level before buying an
      accelerator. Software can request an action; the field node decides.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3 class="sub mono">Stack</h3>
  <p class="note muted">
    Hardware items are the SmartGate design stack: in the design and bench-lab phase, not yet field-proven.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <tbody>
        {
          stack.map((row) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{row.group}</th>
              <td>{row.items.join(' · ')}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h3 class="sub mono">Research interests</h3>
  <p>
    Computer vision and ML for data processing, Internet of Things, Digital Signal Processing, and Cloud-RAN / network
    security for 5G.
  </p>

  <h3 class="sub mono">Languages</h3>
  <p>English (IELTS 6.5) · Vietnamese · French (learning)</p>
</section>

